<section class="block" id="about" aria-labelledby="about-h">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="head"><h2 id="about-h">How this worked</h2></div>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li class="step"><span class="fig">36</span><h3>Starter statements</h3><p>The conversation opened with 36 statements for people to agree or disagree with.</p></li>
      <li class="step"><span class="fig">422</span><h3>People took part</h3><p>Since 2 November they have voted agree, disagree or pass, and added statements of their own.</p></li>
      <li class="step"><span class="fig">2</span><h3>Groups emerged</h3><p>Polis, an open-source tool, grouped people who voted alike. Nobody chose a group. We named them.</p></li>
      <li class="step"><span class="fig">49</span><h3>Voters set priorities</h3><p>On 3 November they spread a budget of credits across 13 possible changes, using quadratic voting.</p></li>
    </ol>
    <details class="fine">
      <summary>Notes for checking the numbers</summary>
      <div class="arows">
        <div class="arow"><h3>Reading the percentages</h3><p>Each figure is the share of a group who agreed, out of those in the group who voted on that statement. Later statements had fewer voters, so where fewer than 20 people in a group voted, we show how many.</p></div>
        <div class="arow"><h3>Reading the map</h3><p>We rebuilt the map from the published votes using the same method as Polis, so people sit close to where the Polis report places them, but not in exactly the same spots. Left to right is the main divide in the votes. Where many people sit close together, their dots pile up, so a dot can sit a little to one side of its exact place.</p><p>Each person’s card shows up to two of their votes as examples. For each group they sided with, it shows the statement most of that group agreed with, preferring statements the person agreed with over ones they rejected.</p></div>
        <div class="arow"><h3>A closer look inside Group 1</h3><p>Inside Group 1, views change sharply near the border with Group 2. The 61 people nearest the border differ from the other 218 on 25 statements. On each, at least 65% agreed on one side and at most 35% on the other, with at least 10 people voting on each side, and a gap that big would happen by chance less than once in a thousand times.</p><p>The eight biggest gaps with at least 20 people voting on each side: families counting as much as economic contribution, 17% of the 61 against 82% of the 218; some of the recent debate being thinly disguised racism, 29% against 91%; migration debates often being an excuse for racism, 27% against 89%; a duty to keep helping people who aren’t so lucky, 23% against 84%; no bias by country of origin in choosing migrants, 22% against 81%; too many people arriving too quickly, 76% against 19%; politics driving the division, and celebrating a multicultural nation, 33% against 90%; migration keeping workplaces and communities going, 25% against 81%. Nearly everyone in both parts enjoys the food, festivals and friendships migration has brought: 96% of the 61 and 99% of the 218.</p><p>When we shuffled the group’s order, no more than one statement ever split it this way. The 61 voted on a median of 34 statements and the 218 on 80, enough to place them.</p><p>Inside Group 2 the same test does find a split, but not one of views. All five people in Group 2 who passed on at least 9 in 10 of the statements they voted on sit near the border, and the test counts a pass as not agreeing. Without those five, only four statements split the group, too few for a closer look.</p><p>We also looked for unlikely allies, people in both groups who back the same statements, and found none that held up.</p></div>
        <div class="arow"><h3>The group cards</h3><p>Each card shows three statements most of the group agreed with and far fewer of the other group did. RadicalxChange chose which three to show.</p></div>
        <div class="arow"><h3>Who and what we counted</h3><p>339 statements were open for voting: the 36 starter statements and 303 written by people taking part. The moderators held back 214 others, so no one else voted on them.</p><p>We count the 422 people who voted on at least one of those 339 statements. Between them they cast 36,371 votes. Polis’s own count of 425 also includes 3 accounts whose only votes were on statements of their own that the moderators held back.</p><p>Six people voted on enough statements to be placed in a group, but didn’t agree or disagree with any of them: every vote they cast was a pass. They count as taking part, but we haven’t placed them in a group, because a pass doesn’t show where someone stands. Polis places them anyway, because it reads a pass as halfway between agree and disagree.</p><p>Six other people passed on at least 9 in 10 of the statements they voted on, 5 of them in Group 2. They agreed or disagreed with at least one, so they stay in the groups Polis placed them in, and their passes count in each group’s figures. That pulls Group 2’s figures down: without them, for example, 90% of Group 2 rather than 81% agreed that too many people have arrived too quickly.</p><p>One person wrote 26 statements in one sitting and voted only on their own; they count as taking part, and Polis placed them in Group 1. Eight others’ only vote was the agreement Polis records when someone adds a statement; they count as taking part but voted on too few statements to be placed in a group.</p></div>
        <div class="arow"><h3>Pairing the statements</h3><p>RadicalxChange chose which statements to pair. Every number comes straight from the results.</p></div>
        <div class="arow"><h3>The priority vote</h3><p>A separate vote with its own voters. We set aside ballots that used fewer than 10 credits (there were none). Where someone voted more than once, we counted the last ballot they submitted. That leaves 49 voters. Net votes are votes for minus votes against. The matching statements from the conversation are close in meaning, not identical in wording.</p><p>The chart shared after the session shows 51 voters, because it counts two names that differ only in capital letters or spacing as two people, and it keeps each person’s fullest ballot rather than their last. The top four are the same either way, in the same order.</p></div>
        <div class="arow"><h3>The data</h3><p>From the Polis export of 18 November 2026, 1:37pm AEDT. The conversation is still open, so these results are preliminary. Statements appear exactly as people wrote them.</p></div>
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="next" aria-labelledby="next-h">
  <div class="wrap next-grid">
    <div>
      <h2 id="next-h">What happens next</h2>
      <p>These results are preliminary. Nicolette Boele and her team will talk them through with residents on 30 November.</p>
